==Abyssal Strategy==
There is not much point in attempting this stage without [[:Category:Uber Rare Cats|Uber Rare Cats]]. It's certainly not impossible, but most strategies are far from practical in any sense of the word. With that being said, the following lineups can act as a decent starting point for building teams.

The general idea for most of these teams is simple:

#Take advantage of the opening solace to push King Wahwah back to the enemy base.
#Let Zang Roo burrow, beat her within an inch of her life, then Holy Blast to permanently get rid of her alongside Scissoroo.
#Kill the Otta-smack-us as fast as possible to reclaim space before Shadow Boxer K shows up.
#Kill Shadow Boxer K before she wrecks you, then clean up the stage.

{{Lineup
|Necro-Dancer Cat
|Skelecat
|Hyper Mr.
|Rodeo Cat
|Lion Cat
|Macho Leg Cat
|name = Lineup 1
|cannon = Holy Blast
}}

'''Battle:''' Lion Cats act as semi-competent meatshields while Macho Leg Cats deal reliable offense. Any backline Ubers that can efficiently crowd-control Zang Roo and Shadow Boxer K are very appreciated here, as they would be covering this lineup's greatest weakness.

{{Lineup
|Quizmaster Cat
|Noodle Cats
|Space Marine Cat
|Puppetmaster & Charley
|Hoopmaster Cat
|Dark Cat
|Dragon Cat
|name = Lineup 2
|cannon = Holy Blast
|notes = Dragon Cat has a lower recharge rate than King Dragon Cat, generally making them the superior option here in spite of the inferior stats (unless you're running an expensive lineup).
}}

'''Battle:''' Dragon Cats deal very high Single Attack damage, and quickly shred through the sparse waves of peons. Where they struggle severely is against the boss, as both are quite sluggish, and the peons can easily prevent them from actually hitting the boss. Rushers that can regain your lost ground and/or stagger King Wahwah are optimal here.

{{Lineup
|The Flying Cat
|Manic Flying Cat
|Flying Ninja Cat
|Time Traveller Cat
|Neo Maneki Cat
|Jamiera Cat
|name = Lineup 3
|cannon = Holy Blast
}}

'''Battle:''' The only thing Flying Cat is good for here is clearing out the Doges and taking hits for Jamiera Cat. They are outranged by everything else outside of Shadow Boxer K (though, they can occasionally strike Scissoroo during her backswing), so refrain from spamming them to save yourself some cash. Any spammable Ubers with good range can basically replace The Flying Cat in this lineup, otherwise, generic backliners that can nuke said threats out of existence are optimal.

{{Lineup
|Li'l Eraser Cat
|Cameraman Cat
|Li'l Jamiera Cat
|Cyborg Cat
|Eraser Cat
|Island Cat
|name = Lineup 4
|cannon = Holy Blast
|notes = You can theoretically swarm King Wahwah and stunlock him to death at the enemy base, eliminating him from the fight. This strategy will not be covering said method due to the exorbitantly high Island Cat, Iron Foundation, and Thunderbolt cannon levels required to pull it off. [https://youtu.be/Ro3541kIFN4 See here].
}}

Seeing as you are graced with Eraser Cat here, this is the most defensively gifted lineup of the bunch. You're able to hold off swarms of enemies very well, however, offense is a major issue. Island Cat is outranged by every relevant threat, which means you need Ubers that can make up that damage during each wave of the fight. Trait-specific Nukers/DPS are effectively mandatory. [[Giga-Nyan Rex (Uber Rare Cat)|Giga-Nyan Rex]] in particular is a good fit for this lineup.

{{Lineup
|Miss Moneko
|Miss NENEKO
|Li'l King Dragon Cat
|Jiangshi Cat
|Mohawk Cat
|True Superfeline
|name = Lineup 5
|cannon = Holy Blast
}}

'''Battle:''' Contrasting Eraser Cat's lineup, this strategy is all offense with minimal defense. True Superfeline deals insanely high Omni Strike damage, eliminating most peons from existence while heavily damaging the Kang Roo variants with each rebound. Unfortunately, Mohawk Cat struggles at holding the lines. Tankers that can protect True Superfeline will ensure he gets the most possible value.

